</div><noscript><p class="note-js">This calculator needs JavaScript. Turn it on, or call (844) 537-1427 and we’ll run the numbers with you.</p></noscript>
<p class="hint">For planning only. Results depend on what you enter and on your policy, and aren’t a quote, an appraisal or legal advice.</p></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>The dates on the calendar</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Notice.</strong> Policies ask for prompt notice of a loss. Late reporting is one of the most common reasons claims get harder.</li>
<li><strong>Temporary repairs.</strong> Tarping and water extraction protect the property and the claim; keep every receipt.</li>
<li><strong>Proof of loss.</strong> If the insurer asks for a sworn proof of loss, many policies allow 60 days from the request.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation recovery.</strong> Replacement-cost policies usually set a window to finish repairs and claim the holdback. Check the number of days in yours.</li>
<li><strong>Appraiser selection.</strong> Many appraisal clauses give each side about 20 days after a written demand to name its appraiser. Enter the demand date to add it.</li>
<li><strong>Suit limitation.</strong> Many Ohio homeowner policies limit lawsuits to one year after the loss, and Ohio courts generally enforce those clauses. Appraisal doesn’t automatically pause that clock.</li></ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Not legal advice</b>The calendar uses the numbers you enter. Courts can read policy deadlines differently. If a date is close, talk to an Ohio attorney now rather than later.</div>
<p>Related: <a href="/appraisal-timeline/">how the appraisal timeline works</a>.</p>
<h2>Questions about this tool</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is the date of loss the day I found the damage?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Usually it is the date of the storm or event, not the day you noticed it. Weather records for your address can help pin it down.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does demanding appraisal stop the one-year suit clock?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Not automatically. Some policies or written agreements address it, many don’t. Treat the suit date as live and get legal advice before it gets close.</p></div></details><details><summary>What does the .ics file work with?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Google Calendar, Apple Calendar and Outlook all import .ics files. The file is built in your browser and never sent to us.</p></div></details></div><h2>More free tools</h2><div class="rel-grid"><a class="rel" href="/appraisal-workbench/appraisal-readiness-checkup/"><small>Checklist</small><span>Appraisal Readiness Checkup</span><svg class="ar" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a class="rel" href="/appraisal-workbench/basement-backup-sublimit-calculator/"><small>Calculator</small><span>Basement Backup Sublimit Calculator</span><svg class="ar" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a class="rel" href="/appraisal-workbench/claim-gap-fee-estimator/"><small>Estimator</small><span>Claim Gap &amp; Fee Estimator</span><svg class="ar" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-box side-call"><h2 c